1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:06,800
Aïcha’s market: the international standards that keep meat safe.

2
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:13,900
Aïcha runs a small abattoir. Buyers from a big coastal city want her meat.

3
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:18,400
But they ask for a guarantee: is this meat safe?

4
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:26,200
The city is in a neighbouring country, which sets its own health rules for the meat it lets in.

5
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:33,600
If every country invented its own rules, every border would ask for something different.

6
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:42,200
That is why countries agreed on common principles, at the World Trade Organization.

7
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:48,000
The WTO SPS Agreement starts with a right:

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:56,200
every country may protect the life and health of people, animals and plants.

9
00:00:56,900 --> 00:01:04,000
But this right has limits. A measure must rest on scientific principles and evidence.

10
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:09,800
It applies only as far as needed to protect health.

11
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:16,400
It must not discriminate between countries where the same conditions prevail.

12
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:22,300
And it must never be a hidden barrier to trade.

13
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:30,400
To choose a measure, a country first assesses the risk, using methods from the international organisations.

14
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:38,200
It weighs the scientific evidence, how animals are raised and processed, and which diseases are present.

15
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:46,200
Then it picks a measure that protects enough, without restricting trade more than required.

16
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:54,000
To bring rules closer, the Agreement asks countries to base their measures on international standards.

17
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Three bodies write them: the Codex Alimentarius for food, WOAH for animal health and the IPPC for plants.

18
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:12,600
A measure that follows these standards is presumed necessary and consistent with the Agreement.

19
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:21,800
A country may protect more strictly than the standard, but it needs a scientific justification.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:26,800
A cow was given an antibiotic yesterday. What does Aïcha do?

21
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Answer 2: respect the withdrawal period to avoid residues in the meat.

22
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:38,000
Before slaughter, the veterinarian inspects the live animals.

23
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:43,800
After slaughter, he inspects each carcass and applies the inspection stamp.

24
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:49,800
Clean premises, clean water, work clothes: hygiene protects the consumer.

25
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:56,800
The buyers’ country does not check meat exactly the way Aïcha’s country does.

26
00:02:56,800 --> 00:03:04,400
Under Article 4, the importer must accept different measures as equivalent,

27
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:13,600
if the exporting country objectively shows that they reach the importer’s level of protection.

28
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:22,000
The exporter supplies the scientific and technical data, and gives access for inspections and tests.

29
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:29,800
On request, the importer explains the risk its measure targets, and normally replies within six months.

30
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:37,600
Meanwhile, trade already under way goes on: a request is no reason to stop it.

31
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:46,000
One morning, foot-and-mouth disease is reported in a region far from Aïcha’s.

32
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:55,600
Must the whole country stop selling? Article 6 says measures must fit the health situation of each area.

33
00:03:56,300 --> 00:04:00,600
What should Aïcha’s country do?

34
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:06,800
Answer 2: report the outbreak, control the zone and prove the other areas are free.

35
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:14,000
The outbreak is reported to WOAH and the zone is put under control.

36
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:20,600
With surveillance and checks, the country proves the other areas are free of the disease,

37
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:26,600
and buyers can keep accepting meat from those areas.

38
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:34,600
Checks have rules too. Inspections must be carried out without undue delay,

39
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:41,000
and imported products are treated no less favourably than local ones.

40
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:48,800
Rules must also be known: each country publishes its measures and notifies changes to the WTO,

41
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:56,600
and runs an enquiry point that answers questions about them.

42
00:04:57,300 --> 00:05:02,000
The meat is ready to leave for the city.

43
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:10,200
How should the meat travel to the city?

44
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:15,500
In a refrigerated truck, the cold chain is never broken.

45
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:22,200
Rules based on the same international standards make trade between countries easier.

46
00:05:22,700 --> 00:05:31,800
Another international text protects the youngest: the WHO International Code of Marketing of Breast-milk Substitutes.

47
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:40,800
It protects breastfeeding: these products should not be advertised to the public, and mothers should not get free samples.

48
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:48,000
And their labels should state that breastfeeding is best.

49
00:07:00,700 --> 00:07:10,000
Four principles to remember: science, common standards, equivalence and disease-free zones.

50
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:19,400
Shared standards for safe meat and open markets.
